+# Decision: Realtime Change Delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must deliver committed stall-status changes to as many as 800 simultaneous staff browsers. Connected browsers must receive changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, opening-time bursts may reach 20 edits per second, and a browser returning after a disconnection of up to 20 minutes must recover the latest board. Subsecond delivery is useful for coordinators even though it is not required by the freshness target.
+
+Edit commands remain ordinary HTTPS requests, and the server assigns every committed edit one authoritative event revision. The delivery mechanism therefore distributes revisions; it does not decide ordering or create a second source of board state.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Major: delivery control and provider coupling.** The realtime path should remain under project control rather than depend on a provider-specific publication and subscription model.
+- **Major: update latency.** Connected browsers should see committed changes promptly enough for staff coordinating activity at opening time.
+- **Moderate: operational burden.** The team must operate the selected mechanism, including its failure and recovery modes.
+- **Moderate: recurring delivery cost.** The mechanism should avoid a recurring third-party realtime fee whose cost grows with connections or messages.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+**Description:** Each browser sends a request every five seconds with its latest event revision. The server returns newer revisions, if any. The change store supports cursor queries, and delivery remains stateless request/response infrastructure. Browsers run a polling loop and surface request failures.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Strong fit for delivery control and provider coupling:** polling uses the project's existing HTTPS boundary and introduces no realtime provider.
+- **Partial fit for update latency:** changes normally wait for the next polling interval, although the five-second interval remains within the 15-second freshness target.
+- **Strong fit for operational burden:** ordinary short-lived requests avoid persistent-session capacity and reconnect protocols.
+- **Strong fit for recurring delivery cost:** there is no third-party realtime fee, although repeated reads consume application and store capacity even when no changes exist.
+
+**Summary:** This is the simplest operational model, paid for with repeated reads and several seconds of avoidable delivery lag.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description:** Browsers hold WebSocket connections to the project's delivery service. The change store retains replayable event revisions and hands committed revisions reliably to delivery. Delivery manages sessions and fans revisions out to connected browsers. A browser reconnects after a failure and catches up from its last applied revision.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Strong fit for delivery control and provider coupling:** the project owns the protocol, session behavior, and publication path without a provider-specific subscription contract.
+- **Strong fit for update latency:** delivery can fan out a committed revision immediately instead of waiting for the next browser request.
+- **Weak fit for operational burden:** the project must size persistent connections and implement liveness, disconnect detection, reconnect, catch-up, and reliable publication.
+- **Strong fit for recurring delivery cost:** it avoids a third-party realtime fee, while adding self-hosted connection and traffic capacity to existing infrastructure costs.
+
+**Summary:** This provides fast, provider-independent delivery in exchange for owning persistent-connection and recovery operations.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+**Description:** The application publishes committed revisions through a managed realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through the provider's client model. The provider operates connection capacity and reconnect transport. The application still reconciles publication with committed storage and retains enough revision history for catch-up when publication or subscription continuity is uncertain.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Weak fit for delivery control and provider coupling:** publication, browser subscriptions, credentials, and some recovery behavior depend on provider-specific contracts and tooling.
+- **Strong fit for update latency:** managed persistent connections can deliver committed revisions immediately.
+- **Strong fit for operational burden:** the provider owns most connection capacity and transport-level reconnect operations, leaving the project to operate publication reconciliation and application-level catch-up.
+- **Weak fit for recurring delivery cost:** the service introduces recurring fees tied to the provider's pricing model.
+
+**Summary:** This provides fast delivery with the least connection operations, paid for through recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses self-hosted WebSockets for committed change delivery. It is the only option with a strong fit for both major criteria: it provides immediate fan-out while keeping the realtime path under project control. HTTPS polling meets the formal freshness goal and is operationally simpler, but accepts repeated reads and avoidable lag during the periods when coordinators need the board most. A hosted realtime service provides comparable latency with less connection work, but gives up control of the subscription path and adds recurring provider fees.
+
+This selection accepts a weaker fit on operational burden. The change store must retain replayable revisions and publish committed changes reliably; delivery must own persistent-session capacity, fan-out, liveness, and server-side recovery behavior; browsers must expose connection failures, reconnect, and catch up from their last applied revision. These obligations preserve the tenets that every committed edit has one authoritative revision and that connectivity failures remain visible rather than appearing to be an unchanged board.
